-- title:
--   Cusp ∞ lies on the Gauss component, read by q-expansions
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$, an integer $M$ with $5 \le M$ and $p \nmid M$, a characteristic-zero field $L$ that is a cyclotomic extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ of type $\{p\}$, and a primitive $p$-th root of unity $\zeta \in L$. Let $K$ be the intermediate field of $L \subseteq \mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,L$ obtained by adjoining to $L$ the coefficientwise image of the $X_1(Mp)$ function field $\subseteq \mathbb{Q}((q))$, let $A$ be a discrete valuation domain with fraction field $L$ such that $p$ lies in its maximal ideal and $\zeta$ is in the image of $A$, with a compatible $A$-algebra structure on $K$, and let $j \in K$ be nonzero with Laurent expansion the image of the $q$-expansion $\mathrm{jq}$. Let $k$ be an algebraically closed $A$-algebra field of characteristic $p$; let $c_1 : C_1 \to \operatorname{Spec} k$, $c_2 : C_2 \to \operatorname{Spec} k$ be proper, smooth of relative dimension $1$ and geometrically integral, and let $i_1, i_2$ be closed immersions of $C_1, C_2$ over $\operatorname{Spec} k$ into the base change to $k$ of the two-chart model $\mathrm{modelTo}\,A\,K\,j$, whose images cover that base change; assume $C_1 \times C_2$ (the fibre product over the model) is reduced, of cardinality $n > 0$. Let $\varepsilon$ be a section of $\mathrm{modelTo}\,A\,K\,j$ over $\operatorname{Spec} A$, let $\varepsilon_1, \varepsilon_2$ be sections of $c_1, c_2$, with $\varepsilon_1$ followed by $i_1$ equal to the base change of $\varepsilon$ to $k$, and let $w$ be an integral weight-one form for $k$ of level $M$, i.e. a weight-one modular form on $\Gamma_1(M)$ together with an integral $q$-expansion whose reduction over $k$ is nonzero. Assume finally that $\varepsilon$ is the cusp: there is a ring homomorphism $\psi$ from the chart algebra $\mathrm{chartAlgInf}\,A\,K\,j$ (the $A$-subalgebra of $K$ generated by $j^{-1}$) to $A$ which retracts $A$, kills $j^{-1}$, and sends $f$ to the $0$-th Laurent coefficient of $f$; that $\varepsilon$ is $\operatorname{Spec}\psi$ followed by the inclusion $\iota_{\infty}$ of that chart; and that the image of $\varepsilon$ misses the range of the finite chart inclusion $\iota_{\mathrm{fin}}$. The conclusion asserts the existence of curve models $\mathrm{Mdl}_1, \mathrm{Mdl}_2$ over $k$ of the Igusa function field $\mathrm{igusaFunctionFieldX1C}\,k\,M\,w$ — each an integral scheme, proper and smooth of relative dimension $1$ over $\operatorname{Spec} k$, with an isomorphism of that field onto its function field compatible with $k$, a bijection between closed points and places whose stalks are the corresponding valuation subrings, and every finite set of points inside an affine open — together with isomorphisms $e_1 : \mathrm{Mdl}_1.C \cong C_1$ and $e_2 : \mathrm{Mdl}_2.C \cong C_2$ compatible with the structure morphisms, and, for $C_1$ only, the following reading by $q$-expansions: the open subscheme of $\mathrm{Mdl}_1.C$ obtained as the preimage under $e_1$ followed by $i_1$ followed by the first projection of the open image of $\iota_{\mathrm{fin}}$ is nonempty, and for every $a$ in $\mathrm{chartAlgFin}\,A\,K\,j$ (the $A$-subalgebra of $K$ generated by $j$) and all $x, y \in A[[q]]$ with $y$ having nonzero image in $k[[q]]$ and $a \cdot y = x$ as Laurent series over $L$, the element of the Igusa function field corresponding, via $\mathrm{Mdl}_1.\mathrm{ffEquiv}$, to the germ in the function field of the pullback of $a$ along that composite equals, inside $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,k$, the quotient of the image of $x$ in $k[[q]]$ by the image of $y$.
--
--   This is the resolution, by the position of the cusp, of the pinned identification of the two components of the geometric special fibre of the two-chart stable model of $X_1(Mp)$: the component carrying the reduction of $\infty$ is the Gauss branch, and its Igusa-function-field coordinates are computed by reducing $A[[q]]$-presentations of functions on the $j$-finite chart. It is used in the assembly of the $X_1(Mp)$ semistable-specialisation family, where every $\infty$-side datum is keyed on this component.
